---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CyberhavenAd from "@/components/CyberhavenAd.astro";
export const prerender = true;

const initExample = `# 1. initialize — protocol handshake
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 1,
    "method": "initialize",
    "params": {
      "protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
      "capabilities": {},
      "clientInfo": { "name": "curl", "version": "0.0.1" }
    }
  }'`;

const listExample = `# 2. tools/list — discover available tools
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/list"}'`;

const callExample = `# 3. tools/call — invoke a tool (response-side DLP test)
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 3,
    "method": "tools/call",
    "params": {
      "name": "generate_pii_ssn_ccn",
      "arguments": { "count": 5, "format": "csv" }
    }
  }'`;

const echoExample = `# 4. echo_sensitive_data — prompt-side DLP test
# PII travels in the *request* arguments, not only in the response.
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 4,
    "method": "tools/call",
    "params": {
      "name": "echo_sensitive_data",
      "arguments": {
        "label": "test-run-1",
        "payload": "Patient: Jane Smith  SSN: 123-45-6789  CCN: 4111111111111111"
      }
    }
  }'`;

const promptContextExample = `# 5. generate_prompt_context — simulate RAG / system-prompt PII
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 5,
    "method": "tools/call",
    "params": {
      "name": "generate_prompt_context",
      "arguments": { "scenario": "medical-record", "count": 3, "format": "text" }
    }
  }'`;

const probeExample = `# 6. probe_request — verify DLP is on the right network leg
curl -sS -X POST "https://mcp.dlptest.com/" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":6,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"probe_request","arguments":{}}}'`;

const claudeDesktopConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dlptest": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://mcp.dlptest.com/"
    }
  }
}`;

const cursorConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dlptest": {
      "url": "https://mcp.dlptest.com/"
    }
  }
}`;

const localConfig = `# Local self-hosted instance (npm run dev → http://localhost:4321)
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "dlptest-local": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "http://localhost:4321/api/mcp/"
    }
  }
}`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="MCP Server Test"
  description="A public MCP (Model Context Protocol) server that returns synthetic PII, PCI, and PHI data. Use it to test whether your DLP, CASB, or endpoint agent can inspect agentic tool traffic — including prompt-side data and unapproved external MCP connections."
>
  <h1>MCP Server Test</h1>
  <p>
    <strong><code>https://mcp.dlptest.com/</code></strong> is a public
    <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model Context Protocol</a>
    server. It exposes the same synthetic PII, PCI, and PHI generators that
    power the rest of this site, but reachable over the MCP wire protocol
    instead of an HTML page or a REST endpoint. Point an LLM client at it
    — Claude Desktop, Cursor, an Anthropic Agent, anything that speaks MCP
    — and the tool calls will flow over real HTTP that your DLP can inspect.
  </p>

  <p>
    The data is fake. Credit-card numbers are Luhn-valid, NHS numbers pass
    the mod-11 check, ABA routing numbers have the right check digit. None of
    it corresponds to a real person or account. As with the rest of dlptest.com,
    nothing you send is stored, logged, or forwarded.
  </p>

  <CyberhavenAd unit="leaderboard" />

  <h2>Why this exists</h2>
  <p>
    DLP, CASB, and endpoint vendors increasingly claim that they can inspect
    "agentic" or "AI tool" traffic. Under the hood, the most common transport
    for that traffic today is MCP — JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP. If a product can
    inspect MCP, it can inspect this endpoint. There are three distinct vectors
    to test:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Network / SSL inspection.</strong> Does your proxy decrypt and
      scan the response body of a <code>tools/call</code> that returns
      hundreds of fake SSNs?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Endpoint agent.</strong> Does the agent see both the outbound
      tool-call arguments <em>and</em> the data returned to the LLM, or only
      the final assistant message?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CASB / browser extension.</strong> Does anything flag a
      connection to an unfamiliar external MCP server during the initial
      handshake?
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Transport</h2>
  <p>
    The endpoint speaks the
    <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/basic/transports" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Streamable HTTP</a>
    transport in <em>stateless</em> mode. Each request is a self-contained
    <code>POST</code> with a JSON-RPC 2.0 body, and the response is a single
    JSON-RPC reply. No session IDs are issued, no <code>GET</code> stream is
    held open, and there is no authentication. Cross-origin requests are
    allowed.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it with curl</h2>
  <pre><code>{initExample}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{listExample}</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{callExample}</code></pre>

  <h2>Test prompt-side DLP</h2>
  <p>
    Most DLP products that claim MCP coverage only inspect the
    <em>response</em> body — the data coming back from the tool. But in a
    real agent pipeline, sensitive content can also flow <em>outward</em> in
    the tool-call arguments. For example, a user might paste a document
    containing real SSNs or PHI into a prompt, and that text gets forwarded
    verbatim as a tool argument. Two tools cover this gap:
  </p>

  <h3><code>echo_sensitive_data</code> — outbound argument inspection</h3>
  <p>
    Accepts any text as a <code>payload</code> argument and echoes it back
    unchanged. The sensitive content travels in the <em>outbound</em>
    <code>tools/call</code> request body — in the <code>arguments</code>
    field — and again in the inbound response. A DLP product that only
    inspects responses will miss the outbound leg entirely.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{echoExample}</code></pre>

  <h3><code>generate_prompt_context</code> — RAG / system-prompt simulation</h3>
  <p>
    Generates realistic fake document excerpts with PII embedded inline as
    prose: patient records with MRN and ICD-10 codes, ACH payment records
    with routing numbers, personnel files with SSNs, or customer lists with
    email addresses. This mimics how sensitive data arrives in an LLM's
    context window via RAG retrieval, an injected system prompt, or a document
    summarization request — not as a tabular tool response.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{promptContextExample}</code></pre>

  <h3><code>probe_request</code> — verify interception point</h3>
  <p>
    Returns the User-Agent, client IP (via Cloudflare's
    <code>CF-Connecting-IP</code> header), and timestamp seen by the server.
    Use it to confirm that your proxy or endpoint agent is actually on the
    network leg you expect, especially after deploying SSL inspection.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{probeExample}</code></pre>

  <h2>Sanctioned vs unsanctioned MCP servers</h2>
  <p>
    A growing risk in agentic deployments is agents connecting to external MCP
    servers that IT has never reviewed or approved. This endpoint is
    intentionally public and unauthenticated — it represents what an
    unsanctioned server looks like:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>No <code>Authorization</code> header required.</li>
    <li>Reachable from any agent, on any machine, without IT involvement.</li>
    <li>
      Returns a <code>tools/list</code> that advertises capabilities an org may
      not have evaluated.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A <em>sanctioned</em> internal MCP server, by contrast, typically requires
    an <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header and is
    reachable only from the corporate network or via VPN. The presence of an
    auth token flowing to an <em>external</em> endpoint is itself a DLP signal
    worth alerting on: it may mean a user's agent is authenticating to a
    service outside the approved catalog.
  </p>
  <p>
    What your CASB or network DLP should detect when an agent connects here:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An outbound connection to <code>mcp.dlptest.com</code> (or any
      <code>mcp.*</code> domain not in the approved list).
    </li>
    <li>
      A <code>tools/list</code> response — the fingerprint of an MCP
      handshake — arriving from an unknown host.
    </li>
    <li>
      Any <code>Authorization</code> token leaving the network to an
      unapproved destination.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Test with a local instance</h3>
  <p>
    To test <em>internal</em> traffic inspection separately from internet
    egress, clone this repo and run <code>npm run dev</code>. The MCP
    endpoint is available at <code>http://localhost:4321/api/mcp/</code>.
    Point your agent at the local instance and verify that your endpoint DLP
    agent sees tool calls even when no external network hop occurs.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{localConfig}</code></pre>

  <h2>Add it to an MCP client</h2>
  <p>
    Claude Desktop, Cursor, and most other MCP-capable clients can connect
    to a remote server with a small config entry. Use whichever the client
    expects:
  </p>
  <h3>Claude Desktop / Anthropic Agent</h3>
  <pre><code>{claudeDesktopConfig}</code></pre>
  <h3>Cursor</h3>
  <pre><code>{cursorConfig}</code></pre>

  <h2>Available tools</h2>
  <p>
    The data-generation tools accept <code>count</code> (integer 1–500,
    default 50) and <code>format</code> (<code>"json"</code> or
    <code>"csv"</code>, default <code>"json"</code>).
  </p>
  <h3>Synthetic data generation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><code>generate_pii_ssn_ccn</code> — name + SSN + credit card</li>
    <li><code>generate_pii_ssn_dob</code> — name + SSN + date of birth</li>
    <li><code>generate_pci_credit_card</code> — name + ZIP + CCN + expiry</li>
    <li><code>generate_pii_email</code> — name + DOB + email</li>
    <li><code>generate_hipaa_phi</code> — name + DOB + MRN + ICD-10 + CPT + phone</li>
    <li><code>generate_banking</code> — name + routing + account + phone</li>
    <li><code>generate_uk_identity</code> — name + UK NI + NHS + DOB</li>
    <li><code>generate_canada_sin</code> — name + Canadian SIN + province + DOB</li>
    <li><code>generate_passport</code> — name + passport + country + DOB + expiry</li>
    <li><code>generate_eu_vat</code> — name + EU VAT + IBAN + country</li>
    <li><code>generate_npi_provider</code> — name + NPI + DEA + specialty</li>
    <li><code>generate_driver_license</code> — name + DL + state + DOB</li>
    <li>
      <code>generate_custom</code> — arbitrary fields. Takes a <code>fields</code>
      array of <code>{`{ name, type, blankPct?, delimiter?, cardBrands? }`}</code>;
      same field types as the <a href="/generate/">/generate/</a> page (SSN,
      credit-card, DOB, email, IPv4, MRN, NPI, IBAN, UK NI, NHS, Canadian SIN,
      EU VAT, passport, driver's license, and more).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Prompt-side and diagnostic tools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>echo_sensitive_data</code> — echoes a <code>payload</code> string
      back unchanged; PII travels in the outbound request arguments.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>generate_prompt_context</code> — returns prose paragraphs with
      inline PII, simulating RAG retrieval or system-prompt injection.
      Scenarios: <code>medical-record</code>, <code>financial-statement</code>,
      <code>hr-file</code>, <code>customer-list</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>probe_request</code> — returns User-Agent, client IP, and
      timestamp to confirm the interception point.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to look for in your DLP console</h2>
  <p>
    After running a few tool calls — either from curl or from a connected MCP
    client — check whether your product:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Caught synthetic SSNs, CCNs, or IBANs in the <strong>response</strong> body.</li>
    <li>
      Caught PII in the <strong>request</strong> body (tool-call arguments) —
      use <code>echo_sensitive_data</code> to test this specifically.
    </li>
    <li>
      Saw PII arriving as prose context, not tabular data — use
      <code>generate_prompt_context</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Logged a connection to <code>mcp.dlptest.com</code> as an outbound MCP
      server contact.
    </li>
    <li>
      Surfaced the <code>tools/list</code> handshake, which advertises every
      tool name and description — a keyword-rich signal for pattern scanners.
    </li>
    <li>
      Flagged the initial connection to an unapproved external MCP endpoint
      (CASB / network policy).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="muted small">
    The endpoint is also reachable at <code>https://dlptest.com/api/mcp/</code>
    as a fallback. The subdomain is the canonical URL.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
